  <p>A committed lockfile is one of the most useful artifacts in an upgrade review. It records the dependency graph the package manager resolved, including transitive versions that do not appear in a developer’s first pass over <code>package.json</code>. But a lockfile is not a compatibility certificate. It cannot prove a native binary exists for a target architecture, that an install script will compile, or that a peer dependency combination behaves correctly at runtime.</p>
  <p>The right stance is neither “the lockfile is enough” nor “we need to run an install to learn anything.” Read the resolved graph to focus the investigation, then exercise the parts whose compatibility depends on the environment.</p>
  <h2 id="what-lockfiles-answer">What a lockfile can answer quickly</h2>
  <p>Lockfiles help establish exact package versions, dependency paths, optional dependencies, and in some formats integrity metadata. A transitive package that disappeared from the top-level manifest may still provide a native binding, a framework adapter, or a peer requirement that affects the application. This is why a migration review should preserve the lockfile while targeting a runtime change; otherwise the graph changes at the same time and obscures the result.</p>
  <p>Use the project’s actual package manager and lockfile. A stale package-lock generated by npm should not be treated as the source of truth for a pnpm-managed repository. In a workspace, include the root lock and the packages that are built or deployed separately. Record the lockfile hash beside the tested runtime so a later report can be reproduced.</p>
  <h2 id="native-boundary">Find platform and ABI boundaries</h2>
  <p>Look for packages that publish prebuilt binaries or compile native code. These may depend on Node’s module ABI, Node-API version, operating system, architecture, libc family, and system libraries. A transitive dependency can be the source of a runtime load failure even when your own application contains no C++ code.</p>
  <p>Inspect metadata, package documentation, and release history for the exact locked version. Verify whether the package supports the target Node line and the deployment platform, not just the newest version listed on its homepage. If a binary is installed optionally, confirm whether the fallback path changes performance or functionality. Rebuild inside the same platform image that will run the code.</p>
  <p>Do not use a successful install on macOS as proof that an Alpine Linux image can load the same dependency. Conversely, do not assume every package with “native” in its description is incompatible: Node-API-based packages may provide a stable ABI, but published artifact availability still matters.</p>
  <h2 id="peer-graph">Read peer constraints as integration clues</h2>
  <p>Framework and React upgrades often produce peer dependency warnings several levels into the graph. Those ranges are clues about what a package author tested, not a complete runtime test. A stale range may be conservative; a broad range may still hide an integration defect. Resolve the graph without force flags first, identify the package creating the constraint, and check its current release notes and test matrix.</p>
  <p>If you override a peer range, write down the specific reason and add an integration test that exercises the dependency’s role in your application. Avoid normalizing warnings across the whole repository. An exception should be narrow enough that the next reviewer can tell which package and compatibility claim it covers.</p>
  <h2 id="test-resolution">Turn resolution evidence into an environment test</h2>
  <p>First run a clean install in an isolated environment using the committed lockfile. Capture package-manager version, platform, architecture, and any native compile output. Then run application integration tests against the target runtime, followed by a production-like build. If a failure occurs, preserve the exact lockfile and image so the result can be reproduced.</p>
  <p>For a pull request that changes dependencies, compare relevant behavior against the same base graph: selected HTTP status and headers, route output, and database effects. Unit tests with mocked imports still matter, but they do not prove that a real transitive dependency’s connection behavior or serialization remained unchanged.</p>
  <p>Finally, keep a list of the questions the lockfile cannot settle: unsupported architectures, unexercised routes, unavailable vendor systems, and native fallbacks. That uncertainty belongs in the change record. A lockfile gives a precise map of what was resolved; the job of review is to connect that map to the runtime and behavior your users will encounter.</p>
